{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5","id":"msg_bdrk_3g5hmplwnujvtxczpsodixg3lbee5ej3x7xkj7lepcpcria5d3wq","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Verification complete. All four phases pass, with zero divergences found between the C and Rust implementations.\n\n## What the library is\n\n`c_src` is a single 20-line translation unit exposing exactly one function — a slice-by-8 CRC-16 (FLAC frame CRC, poly `0x8005`) driven by eight 256-entry tables:\n\n```c\ntflac_u16 crc16(const tflac_u8 *d, tflac_u32 len, tflac_u16 crc16);\n```\n\n## Results by phase\n\n**Phase A** — three artifacts derived mechanically from the C source:\n- `SYMBOLS.md` — C `.so` exports exactly one symbol, `crc16`; Rust `.so` exports it too. `tflac_crc16_tables` is `static` (internal linkage) so it correctly appears in neither.\n- `ERRORS.md` — **13 rows.** The key mechanical finding: the C has exactly *one* `return` statement, zero asserts, zero null checks, zero range checks, zero error enums. `crc16` is a total function that cannot fail, so no return value can be an error sentinel. Rows are therefore the real boundary/degenerate conditions.\n- `CONFIGS.md` — **18 rows** across 8 derived axes (block threshold, `len % 8` residue, wide-iteration count, seed, per-lane byte value, content shape, call composition, alignment). No `#ifdef`s, no flags, no modes — the whole configuration is the three arguments.\n\n**Phase B** — 18/18 rows pass, including exhaustive sweeps (all 65536 seeds at `len=0`, every byte value in each of the 8 lanes, every length 0–520) and 20 000 randomized trials.\n\n**Phase C** — 13/13 rows pass, including null/wild pointers at `len=0`, the `len >= 8` boundary, and the `while (len--)` unsigned wraparound.\n\n**Phase D** — symbol diff is **empty**; all 37 tests pass under both feature configurations.\n\n## Two things worth flagging\n\n**I did not trust the passing tests.** I mutation-tested the suite by injecting 17 realistic translation bugs. One initially *survived* (`len >= 8` → `len > 8`), so I investigated rather than assuming a blind spot — and confirmed against the C `.so` itself (200 000/200 000 random trials) that the wide path and byte path compute identical CRCs. It's an **equivalent mutant**: any threshold ≥ 8 is observationally identical. A second apparent survivor turned out to be a bug in *my script* — the patcher had matched a code comment, producing a no-op. After fixing both, **17/17 caught, 0 survived.**\n\n**One bug class needed special effort.** A sign-extending `len as i32 as usize` is only detectable at `len >= 0x8000_0000`. I tested it for real using a 2 GiB read-only `MAP_NORESERVE` mapping (read faults resolve to the zero page, so it costs page tables, not RAM) — both libraries agree.\n\nThe Rust translation is **correct as written**; I found no divergence requiring a fix. The tables are byte-identical (2048/2048 values), and the `u16` arithmetic correctly reproduces C's integer-promote-then-truncate semantics. My changes were limited to adding `libloading` to `[dev-dependencies]`, the test suite, the three artifacts, and two driver scripts (`run_all.sh`, `mutation_check.sh`). `c_src/` is untouched, and `src/` is clippy-clean with zero warnings."}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1214,"cache_read_input_tokens":136706,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":1214,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":2,"service_tier":"standard"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"977912f8-61bc-46ec-b4fa-051dff075de0","uuid":"554db6aa-2a74-4490-bc77-f6bf29631823","timestamp":"2026-08-26T20:36:22.372Z"}
